      en: "Hegra, also known as Mada'in Salih, is an ancient archaeological site in the Al-Ula region, which served as the southern capital of the Nabataean civilization. The city flourished in the 1st century BC and was an important hub on the incense and spice trade routes. The area is most famous for its monumental rock-cut tombs, of which there are more than 130, and which are impressive examples of Nabataean architecture. The tombs were decorated with elaborate facades that reflect Greek, Roman, Egyptian, and Mesopotamian influences. Hegra became Saudi Arabia's first UNESCO World Heritage Site in 2008. Archaeological excavations in the area have revealed much not only about the Nabataeans' burial customs but also about their water management systems and daily lives. The site provides insight into their cultural and trade relations with the Roman Empire and other contemporary powers."
